{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Here's what makes Decatur different from everywhere else we serve: you don't just live near water. You live with it.</p>

<p>The Tennessee River doesn't pass by Decatur — it wraps around it. Wheeler Lake sits to the east, the main river channel flows past downtown, and the Wheeler Wildlife Refuge stretches across thousands of acres of wetland just north of town. Water is everywhere. And that water affects your home in ways that homeowners in Huntsville or Madison never have to think about.</p>

<p>Start with the water table. In most of North Alabama, the water table sits well below your foundation. In Decatur, especially in the low-lying areas near Point Mallard or along the river, that water table can rise to just a few feet below ground level during wet periods. When TVA releases water through the dam system after heavy regional rainfall — like they did during Hurricane Helene's aftermath — that water table rises even higher.</p>

<p>What does this mean for your house? It means the soil around your foundation is often saturated from below, even before rain hits your roof. When your gutters overflow or your downspouts dump water too close to the foundation, you're adding more water to soil that can't absorb any more. The result is hydrostatic pressure pushing against your basement walls and moisture creeping into places it shouldn't be.</p>

<p>Then there's the humidity factor. Decatur's river location means higher average humidity than areas just 20 miles inland. That humidity accelerates everything: paint fails faster, fascia boards rot quicker, gutter seams corrode sooner. A gutter system that lasts 20 years in Huntsville might only last 12 in Decatur. We've seen it repeatedly.</p>

<p>The neighborhoods matter too. Old Albany and the historic downtown sit on slightly higher ground — they've been there since before the Civil War for good reason. But the post-war expansion pushed into lower-lying areas that require more aggressive drainage solutions. If you're near Brookmeade, Point Mallard, or parts of Southwest Decatur, you're probably dealing with drainage challenges that standard gutter work won't solve. Underground drains, French drains, sump systems — these aren't luxuries in river-adjacent Decatur. They're necessities.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Living in Decatur means playing by different rules when it comes to home drainage. Here's what river-town homeowners need to know.</p>

<h4>Standard Maintenance Isn't Enough</h4>
<p>The twice-a-year gutter cleaning that works in Huntsville? That's a starting point in Decatur, not the finish line. Decatur's humidity means organic debris breaks down faster, creating that thick, composted sludge that's harder to clear than dry leaves. Clean more often — especially if you're near mature trees — and don't just scoop out the visible stuff. Flush downspouts completely to clear what's building up in the elbows.</p>

<h4>Humidity-Specific Concerns</h4>
<p>That muggy river air does a number on your home's exterior. Here's what to watch:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fascia board rot:</strong> Check the wood behind your gutters annually. Peeling paint is the early warning sign. Once rot sets in, it spreads fast in Decatur's humidity.</li>
<li><strong>Mold and mildew:</strong> Look for dark staining on gutters and soffits. It's not just cosmetic — it indicates moisture problems that will get worse.</li>
<li><strong>Seam corrosion:</strong> Gutter seams fail faster in humid conditions. If you see rust or mineral deposits at joints, you're past due for maintenance or replacement.</li>
<li><strong>Gutter guard performance:</strong> Some guards trap moisture underneath, accelerating roof edge decay. In Decatur, make sure guards allow airflow, not just debris blocking.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Flood Season Preparation</h4>
<p>When heavy rain hits regionally, TVA manages lake levels by releasing water through the dam system. This can raise Decatur's water table within hours. Before flood season (typically March-May):</p>
<ul>
<li>Make sure gutters are clear and flowing freely</li>
<li>Verify downspouts extend at least 6 feet from the foundation — 10 feet is better</li>
<li>Check that underground drains (if you have them) are clear and functional</li>
<li>Know where water pools in your yard and make sure it has somewhere to go that isn't toward your house</li>
</ul>

<h4>Signs of Water Table Issues</h4>
<p>Decatur homeowners should know these warning signs that ground moisture is affecting your foundation:</p>
<ul>
<li>Musty smell in basement or crawl space that appears after rain</li>
<li>Efflorescence (white chalky deposits) on basement walls</li>
<li>Sticking doors or windows that weren't a problem before</li>
<li>Hairline cracks in foundation walls, especially horizontal ones</li>
<li>Sump pump running constantly during wet weather</li>
</ul>

<h4>Underground Drains: Essential, Not Optional</h4>
<p>In much of Decatur, standard gutter-and-downspout systems can't move water far enough from the foundation fast enough. Underground drains — buried pipes that carry water from downspouts to discharge points well away from the house — are often the difference between a dry basement and a chronic moisture problem. If you're within a mile of the river or in a low-lying area, this isn't a luxury upgrade. It's what your home needs.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set local_spotlight %}
<p>Decatur's historic neighborhoods tell the story of a city that's been thriving since the 1820s. The Old State Bank building downtown — the oldest bank structure in Alabama — has watched this city survive the Civil War, rebuild from complete destruction, and grow into the industrial powerhouse it became in the 20th century. The homes along Bank Street and throughout the Albany district carry that same history.</p>

<p>We've worked on dozens of these historic Decatur homes, and here's what we've learned: preserving character while improving function takes a different approach than standard residential work.</p>

<h4>The Industrial Heyday Housing</h4>
<p>Many of Decatur's most distinctive homes were built between the 1920s and 1950s, when the city's industrial economy was booming. These aren't cookie-cutter houses — they're Craftsman bungalows, Tudor revivals, Colonial-style homes, and mid-century ranches with character that new construction can't replicate. They also have gutter systems that range from "original and failing" to "badly modernized and ugly."</p>

<h4>Historic Home Gutter Challenges</h4>
<p>Older construction presents specific problems:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Original gutters:</strong> Some homes still have cast iron or galvanized steel gutters from the 1920s-40s. Beautiful patina, terrible drainage. Rusted through, undersized, and often hanging on by sheer stubbornness.</li>
<li><strong>Early aluminum upgrades:</strong> Many historic homes got basic aluminum gutters in the 1960s-80s. These are now failing — seams separated, hangers pulled loose, sagging everywhere.</li>
<li><strong>Inappropriate modern work:</strong> We've seen historic homes with white K-style gutters slapped on with zero regard for aesthetics. It hurts to look at, and it often doesn't function well either.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden fascia damage:</strong> Behind those old gutters, there's often decades of rot that nobody's seen. We never install new gutters until we know what we're attaching them to.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Matching New to Historic</h4>
<p>The right gutter system for a 1925 Bank Street home isn't the same as for a 2020 suburban build. Options that respect Decatur's architectural heritage:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Half-round gutters:</strong> The traditional profile that belongs on historic homes. Available in copper (which develops beautiful verdigris patina over time) or aluminum painted to match existing trim.</li>
<li><strong>Copper systems:</strong> Premium option that actually improves with age. Higher upfront cost, but lasts generations and looks like it belongs on a historic property.</li>
<li><strong>Custom color matching:</strong> When seamless aluminum is the right choice, we color-match to existing trim rather than defaulting to generic white or brown.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Our Approach</h4>
<p>We take historic Decatur homes seriously. Before we quote anything, we look at the whole picture: the architectural style, what's original versus what's been changed, the condition of fascia and soffits, and how water currently flows (and where it's causing problems). We've worked on enough Albany and downtown homes to know that these projects require extra care — and we give them that care.</p>

<p>Your 1930s Decatur home has survived nearly a century. With the right drainage system, it'll thrive for another century. And it'll still look like it belongs in the neighborhood.</p>
{% endset %}
